  <h1 class="chap-title">Chapter I: Historical Background of Srinagar and Shehr-i-Khaas</h1>
  
  <h2 class="sec-title">1.1 Geographical Setting: The Jhelum River (Vyeth)</h2>
  <p>
    Srinagar, the historic capital of Kashmir, situated at 34&deg;5'24"N and 74&deg;47'24"E at an elevation of approximately 5,200 feet above mean sea level, evolved along the banks of the River Jhelum[span_0](start_span)[span_0](end_span). Known locally as the <em>Vyeth</em>, celebrated in classical Sanskrit literature as the <em>Vitasta</em>, and designated the <em>Hydaspes</em> by classical Greek writers, the river historically structured the settlement pattern and physical morphology of Srinagar[span_1](start_span)[span_1](end_span). Its course determined the distribution of residential quarters, trade depots, waterfront ghats, places of worship, and civic bridges[span_2](start_span)[span_2](end_span)[span_3](start_span)[span_3](end_span).
  </p>
  <p>
    Walter R. Lawrence documented the close relationship between the city and the Jhelum in <em>The Valley of Kashmir</em>[span_4](start_span)[span_4](end_span). Lawrence observed that the city extended along both banks of the river, which had an average width of approximately eighty yards[span_5](start_span)[span_5](end_span). The urban settlement stretched nearly three miles along the fluvial course and covered a breadth of one and a half miles, with the larger share situated on the right bank[span_6](start_span)[span_6](end_span). Beyond physical morphology, the Jhelum was the principal commercial highway and transport route of the valley[span_7](start_span)[span_7](end_span). It formed a navigable waterway from Khanabal down to the gorge of Baramulla, carrying agricultural produce, timber, grain, wool, and handcrafted textiles by flat-bottomed cargo boats (<em>bahats</em>) and light skiffs (<em>shikaras</em>), compensating for the historical lack of paved overland roads[span_8](start_span)[span_8](end_span).
  </p>
  <p>
    The historic core of Srinagar, known as Shehr-i-Khaas, emerged directly out of this riverine setting[span_9](start_span)[span_9](end_span). Houses, bazaars, ghats, and mosques developed along the winding contours of the river[span_10](start_span)[span_10](end_span)[span_11](start_span)[span_11](end_span). Because the Jhelum divided the settlement into two halves, permanent crossings were essential for daily communication[span_12](start_span)[span_12](end_span)[span_13](start_span)[span_13](end_span). The resulting network of wooden bridges—locally known as <em>kadals</em>—became integral links in the urban fabric, connecting commercial, social, and cultural activities across both banks[span_14](start_span)[span_14](end_span)[span_15](start_span)[span_15](end_span).
  </p>
  <h2 class="sec-title">1.2 Foundations and Urban Development: Maurya Era to Pravarapura</h2>
  <p>
    The urban foundations of Srinagar date to the third century BCE[span_16](start_span)[span_16](end_span)[span_17](start_span)[span_17](end_span). Kalhana’s authoritative twelfth-century chronicle, the <em>Rajatarangini</em>, attributes the founding of the capital <em>Srinagari</em> to Emperor Ashoka (reigned c. 268–232 BCE)[span_18](start_span)[span_18](end_span)[span_19](start_span)[span_19](end_span). Ashoka established this settlement at modern Pandrethan, derived from the Sanskrit <em>Puranadhisthana</em>, meaning "Old Capital", situated three miles upstream from modern downtown Srinagar[span_20](start_span)[span_20](end_span). Ashoka built the Dharmaranya Vihara, several stupas, and a chaitya on the banks of the Vitasta[span_21](start_span)[span_21](end_span). Under the subsequent Gonanda dynasty, the city retained its administrative role until a conflagration during the reign of King Abhimanyu (c. 960 CE) destroyed its timber domestic quarters[span_22](start_span)[span_22](end_span).
  </p>
  <p>
    In the sixth century CE, King Pravarasena II shifted the capital downstream, founding <em>Pravarapura</em> centered around Hari Parbat (<em>Koh-i-Maran</em>) on the right bank of the Jhelum[span_23](start_span)[span_23](end_span). Because the ancient capital at Pandrethan and the new seat lay in close proximity, the name <em>Srinagari</em> persisted in popular usage, while <em>Pravarapura</em> gradually faded from colloquial speech[span_24](start_span)[span_24](end_span). Kalhana describes Pravarapura as a thriving urban center with wooden residences, markets, and waterways[span_25](start_span)[span_25](end_span). He notes that Pravarasena II ordered the construction of a large bridge of boats across the Vitasta, establishing cross-river transit early in the city's history[span_26](start_span)[span_26](end_span). When the Chinese pilgrim Xuanzang (Hiuen Tsang) visited Kashmir in 631 CE, he documented that Pravarapura was the active political capital, while Puranadhisthana lay roughly two miles to the southeast[span_27](start_span)[span_27](end_span). Over subsequent centuries, settlement expanded across both banks of the river to form Shehr-i-Khaas[span_28](start_span)[span_28](end_span)[span_29](start_span)[span_29](end_span).
  </p>
  <h2 class="sec-title">1.3 Socio-Economic and Political Nucleus Across Dynastic Regimes</h2>
  <p>
    Under the Kashmir Sultanate (1320–1586), Srinagar developed into a major regional center of Islamic craft, architecture, and administration[span_30](start_span)[span_30](end_span). Sultan Alauddin (1344–1356) established Alauddinpur near Aali Kadal[span_31](start_span)[span_31](end_span). Sultan Zain-ul-Abidin (1420–1470 CE), known as <em>Budshah</em>, laid out the suburb of Naushahr, excavated the Nalla Mar canal, and brought skilled craftsmen from Central Asia to establish trades such as papier-m&acirc;ch&eacute;, wood carving, and silk weaving[span_32](start_span)[span_32](end_span)[span_33](start_span)[span_33](end_span). In 1427, Zain-ul-Abidin built Zaina Kadal, the first recorded permanent wooden cantilever bridge across the Jhelum[span_34](start_span)[span_34](end_span)[span_35](start_span)[span_35](end_span). The religious landscape was anchored by the Jamia Masjid in Nowhatta and the Khanqah-i-Mualla along the right bank of the river[span_36](start_span)[span_36](end_span).
  </p>
  <p>
    Following the Mughal annexation in 1586, Srinagar was integrated into the imperial administrative framework[span_37](start_span)[span_37](end_span). In 1589, Emperor Akbar ordered the construction of the stone-walled citadel of Nagar Nagar around Hari Parbat[span_38](start_span)[span_38](end_span). Later Mughal administrators added civic works: Governor Saif Khan built Safa Kadal in 1671, and Governor Fazil Khan constructed protective bunds to mitigate seasonal floods[span_39](start_span)[span_39](end_span)[span_40](start_span)[span_40](end_span).
  </p>
  <p>
    During Afghan rule (1753–1819), administrative instability affected municipal infrastructure, though Governor Amir Khan Jawansher constructed Amira Kadal and developed the Sher Garhi Palace on the left bank[span_41](start_span)[span_41](end_span)[span_42](start_span)[span_42](end_span). Under Sikh rule (1819–1846), traveler William Moorcroft observed that unpaved lanes, neglected canals, and deteriorating bridge piers reflected significant urban decline[span_43](start_span)[span_43](end_span). Following the establishment of the Dogra dynasty in 1846, systematic municipal works were undertaken[span_44](start_span)[span_44](end_span). After the devastating flood of 1893, Maharaja Pratap Singh sponsored the reconstruction of the major timber bridges (Zaina Kadal in 1897, Habba Kadal in 1898, Safa Kadal in 1901, and Fateh Kadal in 1902), stabilizing downtown traffic networks[span_45](start_span)[span_45](end_span)[span_46](start_span)[span_46](end_span).
  </p>
  <h2 class="sec-title">1.4 The Vernacular Settlement Pattern of Shehr-i-Khaas</h2>
  <p>
    The urban fabric of Shehr-i-Khaas evolved through sustained adaptation to the valley's geography and hydrology[span_47](start_span)[span_47](end_span). It was characterized by five interlinked components:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Narrow Streets and Interconnected Lanes:</strong> Dense alleyways (<em>koochas</em>) optimized pedestrian movement, conserved thermal heat during winters, and linked residential mohallas directly with bridge approaches[span_48](start_span)[span_48](end_span).</li>
    <li><strong>The Canal Network (Nalla Mar):</strong> Excavated under Sultan Zain-ul-Abidin, this canal connected the Jhelum and Dal Lake with Anchar Lake, serving as an internal transport and drainage network until it was filled in for a vehicular road in 1975[span_49](start_span)[span_49](end_span)[span_50](start_span)[span_50](end_span).</li>
    <li><strong>Craft-Specific Bazaars:</strong> Bazaars were organized into specialized occupational quarters (e.g., copperware hammering, spice markets, and shawl weaving), located near bridgeheads to facilitate cargo transit[span_51](start_span)[span_51](end_span)[span_52](start_span)[span_52](end_span).</li>
    <li><strong>Riverfront Ghats and Yarbals:</strong> Flights of stone landing steps along the banks served as active public interfaces for drawing water, domestic washing, loading boats, and neighborhood assembly[span_53](start_span)[span_53](end_span).</li>
    <li><strong>Vernacular Residential Architecture:</strong> Homes utilized seismic-responsive framing systems—<em>Taq</em> (masonry piers tied with horizontal timber runners) and <em>Dhajji-Dewari</em> (timber frames filled with lightweight masonry)—featuring cantilevered wooden bay windows (<em>zoon dab</em>) and pine lattice screens (<em>pinjrakari</em>)[span_54](start_span)[span_54](end_span)[span_55](start_span)[span_55](end_span).</li>
  </ul>
</div>
